  "means": {
    "overview": "These figures are about people who have already had their operation, not people still waiting. The 45-day national figure counts every patient once. The state bars use each state total for waits longer than a year. Nine in ten patients nationally were admitted within 329 days.",
    "states": "A state can look reasonable overall and still miss the urgent clock. New South Wales does well on urgent surgery and poorly on waits over a year, driven by eye surgery: half of those patients waited longer than 224 days, and 20.7% waited more than a year (39,389 operations).",
    "hospitals": "Sydney Hospital / Sydney Eye Hospital has the largest single gap on this list: about 2,365 non-urgent patients were treated outside the recommended time. At Royal Adelaide Hospital the typical urgent patient was admitted within 26 days, inside the 30-day mark, but only 60.0% were. That is about 2,218 people.",
    "urgency": "Semi-urgent surgery is the bulk of the missed clock: 72.0% were done within 90 days, about 86,150 people were not. Ear, nose and throat, eye surgery, and orthopaedics have the longest typical waits. Knee replacement, septoplasty, tonsils, hip replacement, and cataract surgery are the high-volume operations to watch.",
    "actions": "Look first at urgent surgery where the 30-day mark is missed, then at semi-urgent services that miss the 90-day mark for a large number of people, then at the states and operations where more than a year is becoming common. Small hospitals with a handful of operations can post a very long typical wait; the list above weighs that by how many people are affected."
  },
